---
import Base from '~/layouts/Base.astro';
import { totals, REPO } from '~/lib/data';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Who made the Founder Index, why there is no paid placement, how entries get verified, and what licence the code and data are under."
  nav="about"
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>A working notebook, opened up</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        This is a directory of {totals.entries} tools, grants, programmes and things worth
        reading for people starting a company, organised by the stage you're at rather than
        the software category the tool belongs to.
      </p>

      <h2>Why stages</h2>
      <p>
        A first-time founder does not know they need attribution software. They know
        they've launched and nobody is coming back. Category-first directories assume you
        already have the vocabulary for your problem, which is exactly what you don't have
        the first time. So the organising principle here is the question you're actually
        asking this week, and the answer is two or three resources rather than forty.
      </p>
      <p>
        The twelve stages are a rough sequence, not a pipeline. Most people are in three of
        them at once and going backwards is normal — you validate again after you launch,
        you rebrand after you find the real customer.
      </p>

      <h2>Where it came from</h2>
      <p>
        It started as a running list of links accumulated over years of building things —
        the kind of note that grows to hundreds of lines, half of it saved at 2am and never
        opened again. Every entry was used or researched by someone building a real
        company. That's the entire credibility claim, and it's why the design looks like a
        notebook rather than a storefront.
      </p>
      <!--
        PLACEHOLDER: replace with the real name, handle and one honest line about
        who you are. An about page that won't say who wrote it is worth less than
        one that does.
      -->
      <p>
        Maintained in the open. The contributors are whoever has sent a pull request —
        <a href={`${REPO}/graphs/contributors`} rel="noopener">the list is public</a>, like
        everything else here.
      </p>

      <h2>No paid placement, ever</h2>
      <p>
        Nobody has paid to be in this directory, and nobody can. There is no sponsored
        slot, no boosted position, no "featured" tier, and no arrangement where a tool gets
        a better description because of who they are. Ordering is by how many entries a
        category has, or by the order things were added — never by money.
      </p>
      <p>
        If an affiliate or referral link is ever added, it gets an{' '}
        <span class="chip chip--affiliate">affiliate link</span> chip on its card and its own
        line in the repository README. Today there are none. You can verify that yourself:
        the data is <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/data/resources.json`} rel="noopener">one JSON file</a>,
        and the git history shows every change ever made to it.
      </p>

      <h2>Honest about uncertainty</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/unverified">{totals.unverified} of the {totals.entries} entries</a> carry an{' '}
        <span class="chip chip--unverified">unverified</span> chip. That means the link or
        the description was inferred from a name in the original notes and nobody has
        confirmed it since. The tool might be misnamed, dead, or something other than what
        we've assumed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those entries are shown, not hidden, because a guess labelled as a guess is more
        useful than a gap. What they never do is pretend. An entry stops being unverified
        when a human opens the link, confirms the description is true, and adds a{' '}
        <code>lastChecked</code> date in a pull request.
      </p>
      <p>
        A weekly job also pings every link and opens an issue listing anything that has
        started returning an error. Dead links get removed or fixed, not quietly left to
        rot.
      </p>

      <h2>How it's built</h2>
      <p>
        Astro, statically generated, deployed from <code>main</code>. No database, no
        accounts, no server. The data lives in <code>data/resources.json</code> and CI
        validates it on every commit — schema conformance, no duplicate links, no slug
        collisions, and a hard fail on any description over twelve words.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is deliberately no voting, no saved lists and no user accounts. All three
        need moderation, and the repository already is the moderation layer.
      </p>

      <h2>Licence</h2>
      <p>
        The code is <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/LICENSE`} rel="noopener">MIT</a>. The dataset
        is <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/DATA-LICENSE`} rel="noopener">CC BY 4.0</a> — take it,
        build something better with it, just say where it came from. If you fork this into a
        directory for a field I know nothing about, that's the best outcome available.
      </p>

      <p style="margin-top:2.5rem">
        <a class="btn" href={REPO} rel="noopener" target="_blank">The repository</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/submit" style="margin-left:0.5rem">Add a resource</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
